{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And why have you brought the assembly of HaShem.</b> First it says ״וַיִּקָּהֲלוּ עַל מֹשֶׁה וְעַל אַהֲרֹן״ — against both, and afterward it says ״וַיָּרֶב הָעָם עִם מֹשֶׁה״ — against Moses alone, and afterward it returns and says ״וְלָמָה הֲבֵאתֶם״ — to both of them, and afterward ״לָמָה הֶעֱלִיתָנוּ״ — to Moses alone (see Ba'al ha-Turim). And furthermore, they should have placed the complaint of ״וְלָמָה הֶעֱלִיתָנוּ מִמִּצְרַיִם״ before ״וְלָמָה הֲבֵאתֶם אֶת קְהַל ה׳ אֶל הַמִּדְבָּר״, according to the order of their occurrence. And furthermore, that it changed their names several times — from עֵדָה, to קָהָל, and הָעָם, and קְהַל ה׳." "And the explanation for all this is that Israel knew that the well was in the merit of Miriam, and the manna in the merit of Moses, and the clouds of glory in the merit of Aaron, and had they traveled through an inhabited land they would not have needed any of this. And it is known that the bringing up from Egypt — Moses alone was the principal, as it is written (Exodus 32:1): ״כִּי זֶה מֹשֶׁה הָאִישׁ אֲשֶׁר הֶעֱלָנוּ״ etc., but their consenting to enter the wilderness was through both of them, for they could not survive there without manna and without clouds of glory. Therefore it says: ״וַיִּקָּהֲלוּ עַל מֹשֶׁה וְאַהֲרֹן״, and it does not yet specify why they gathered against the two of them. And afterward it says: ״וַיָּרֶב הָעָם עִם מֹשֶׁה״ alone, and it does not yet specify why they quarreled with him. And afterward it specifies each one in order — first the first: that their gathering against the two of them was in order to say to them ״וְלָמָה הֲבֵאתֶם אֶת קְהַל ה׳ אֶל הַמִּדְבָּר״, for the two of them were the cause for them coming into the wilderness. And the last for the last: that what is stated ״וַיָּרֶב הָעָם עִם מֹשֶׁה״ — that is, they said ״וְלָמָה הֶעֱלִיתָנוּ מִמִּצְרַיִם״ etc., for Moses alone brought them up. And as for their placing the complaint of ״וְלָמָה הֲבֵאתֶם״ etc. first — it is because this is the greatest of all, for they would have suffered no harm from the bringing up from Egypt had they not been brought into the wilderness. Therefore they made their complaint first about being brought into the wilderness, which is evil in and of itself, even if they had not departed from an important place like Egypt; and afterward they made a lesser complaint than this — namely that he forced them from one extreme to the other, for he brought them up from Egypt, a place that is like the garden of HaShem for trees and seeds, to this evil place, no place of seed and figs etc." "And according to what we explained above, that they complained because they had lost their portion in the World to Come, it is also fitting why they placed the complaint of ״וְלָמָה הֲבֵאתֶם״ etc. first — for it is indeed the greater one. And this is what it says ״וַיָּבֹאוּ כָּל הָעֵדָה״ — this means the complete assembly, as Rashi explained; ״וַיֵּשֶׁב הָעָם בְּקָדֵשׁ״ — every place where ״הָעָם״ is stated speaks of the wicked, as Rashi explained on the verse ״וַיְהִי הָעָם כְּמִתְאֹנְנִים״ (Numbers 11:1); and it means that the wicked among them sought to settle permanently in Kadesh, and they had no desire or wish to go to the Land, for they hated the Land. Immediately ״וַתָּמָת שָׁם מִרְיָם״ — so that on account of the lack of water they would not seek permanent settlement, and her death was the cause of the well's ceasing, and with it was fulfilled the statement of the Talmud (Bava Kamma 92a), that there was no water for the assembly, even for the whole ones among them who were called by the name ״עֵדָה״. ״וַיָּרֶב הָעָם״ — these are the lesser ones; but the good ones remained silent, looking forward to the salvation of HaShem. And as for their saying ״וְלָמָה הֲבֵאתֶם אֶת קְהַל ה׳״ — this is their own words, for in their own eyes they were worthy."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {2 "The phrase וּבַהֲדֵי הוֹצָא לָקֵי כַּרְבָא — literally 'and with it was fulfilled for us the statement of the rabbi' — rendered as citing the Talmudic dictum; 'for us' is omitted as it appears to be a formulaic citation marker rather than part of the argument."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:42:17.703354567Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}